  "text": "umably $x = a+bi,$ $y = a-bi,$ $z = c.$ Then $|x|^2+|y|^2+|z|^2 = 2a^2 + c^2= 352.$\n\nSince $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ are the roots of the polynomial $Q(z),\\,$ we can use Vieta's formulas to find $s$ and $t.$ Namely,\n\n$$\n\\begin{align*}\nx+y+z&=0,\\\\\nxy+xz+yz&=-s,\\\\\nxyz&=t.\n\\end{align*}\n$$\n\nBut $x+y+z = 0+0+c= c,$ so the coefficients of $Q(z)$ and $P(\\lambda)$ must be equal, hence\n\n$$\nc=s=0, \\,\\, t=0.\n$$\n\nThat is, the polynomial $Q(z)$ is simply a cubic $z^3, \\rm{and}\\,\\, P(\\lambda) = \\lambda^3.$\n\nThe vertices of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ divide the line segment $AB$ into three equal parts, and $C$ is the midpoint of $AB.$ Hence, $BC = \\frac{AB}{3} = \\frac{b}{3}.$\n\nThe triangle is isosceles with base $b.$ So $BC= \\frac{b}{3}, \\rm{and}\\,\\, CA=\\frac{2b}{3}.$ By the Pythagorean theorem, $\\bigl(\\frac{b}{3}\\bigr)^2 = \\frac{1}{3}b^2.= 128.$",
